{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 asserts a SUBSTANTIVE integration relation (resilience depends on matching the failure's causal nature—stochastic vs systemic—to the structural defense—redundancy vs heterogeneity), but it provides no causal mechanism explaining WHY stochastic failures require redundancy rather than heterogeneity or why systemic failures require heterogeneity rather than redundancy. Without specifying the operative mechanism (e.g., redundancy mitigates stochastic failures because independent replication makes simultaneous random failure vanishingly improbable; heterogeneity mitigates systemic failures because diverse implementations avoid common-mode triggers), the proposed dependence is an asserted correspondence, not a mechanistic relation. The 'refuted if' condition merely restates the correspondence in contrapositive and does not supply the missing causal link.","target":"41bbaa098bc69b6e5a39c420349afb677c00b98bcbeb73924988cf150d99c8f4"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 claims a contradiction relation (logical decentralization is negated when physical dependencies intersect), but it supplies no causal mechanism connecting 'dependency intersection' to the collapse of logical decentralization. The target does not define what threshold of intersection triggers systemic collapse, nor why intersecting physical dependencies necessarily negate logical decentralization rather than merely degrading it. The 'refuted if' clause itself betrays this gap: it defers the entire causal content to an unspecified 'threshold required to trigger a systemic collapse,' making the contradiction conditional on a mechanism that is never articulated. A contradiction relation demands a causal account of how physical intersection propagates to logical negation; absent that, the claim is a bare correlation.","target":"9bef64bef1237cf8366dd8f999631048616145a128cafabcbfbeab9e97c9b482"}]}